5. Validation after writing
The builder reopens the output store and computes a small sample statistic. Also check:

expected dimensions, coordinate ranges and time count;
data-variable names and attributes/units;
valid-value coverage in a few known maps and time slices;
representative values against the interim NetCDF source;
whether map/time-series reads perform acceptably for your use case.

A successful Zarr write checks storage/readability, not the scientific correctness of source values or remapping.

This notebook has been designed to create some nice plots to explore the data. This will allow you to see what did you start with and what is the output now.
from pathlib import Path

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import xarray as xr


## function to find the root of the repository, which is assumed to contain a "data" directory
def find_repo_root() -&gt; Path:
    for candidate in [Path.cwd(), *Path.cwd().parents]:
        if (candidate / "data").is_dir():
            return candidate
    raise FileNotFoundError("Could not find a repository containing data/")

### I am using Metref as the default variable to plot, but you can change it to any other variable in the dataset. 
def select_plot_variable(ds: xr.Dataset) -&gt; str:
    preferred = ["METREF", "metref"]
    for name in preferred:
        if name in ds.data_vars:
            return name
    numeric = [name for name, da in ds.data_vars.items() if da.dtype.kind in "iuf"]
    if not numeric:
        raise ValueError("Dataset has no numeric data variable to plot")
    return numeric[0]

### This is for quick snapshot plotting - using time = 0, by default. You can change the time index to plot other time slices.
def plot_snapshot(ds: xr.Dataset, title: str):
    variable = select_plot_variable(ds)
    da = ds[variable]
    if "time" in da.dims:
        da = da.isel(time=0)
    print(f"{title}: plotting {variable}, shape={da.shape}")
    return da.plot(figsize=(10, 5), robust=True, cmap="viridis")


REPO_ROOT = find_repo_root()
DATA_ROOT = REPO_ROOT / "data"

6. Check for missing values
Compare raw and processed summaries and map the output. An all-NaN or mostly missing result can be caused by:

crop bounds and target-grid bounds not overlapping;
AOI partly outside raw source coverage (output is limited to its overlap);
wrong variable, coordinate names or coordinate direction;
fill values not decoded as missing;
a source mask or retrieval-quality exclusion;
invalid or unsupported remapping setup.

Some missing values are expected. Set an acceptable valid-coverage threshold for the application, and check coverage spatially as well as numerically. A file existing or opening is not enough to establish fitness for use.
